# Babble seed (2026-11-16)
#
# Threads from one conversation between the owner and a workbench session, written down so design agents start with them.
# Three kinds of line: the owner's words, what was measured here, and babble. Babble is a claim to test, never a finding.

#>babble_seed_2026_11_16
#:journal #!babble
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"rules"{
 arms: "The working reading of side: a mark before a name acts before the name is read; a mark after it acts on what the name yields. The mirror reading was not taken."
 never_one: "Two marks are never read as one operator. A run of marks is its marks in turn."
 chaining: "Whether marks may chain, and how, is a setting of each dialect and project."
 corpus: "Do not assume the existing corpus or today's feature usage is ideal; it could not use what was never handled."
 compatibility: "Compatibility is not very important this year."
 wonders: "Ideas are open wonders. Only the owner judges; nothing here is decided."
 names_first: "People who have not bought in to the language care about names they can relate to and the relationships between them."
}

^"owner"{
 # The owner's words, 2026-11-16 unless dated.
 surface: "prefix/postfix differentiation and complex expressions like =foo@(<bar>) would provide the strongest surface for novel linguistic wonder into lore.land and spw.quest through factshift.center; I want Spw to provide interactive surfaces for extending wonder about pivoting between nuances about the symbolic or semantic space around each fragment of an idea"
 portable: "aim to make the tooling intermediate forms more portable across contexts when Spw forms are selected"
 babble: "I'm interested in treating this a bit like a babbling session"
 geometry: "the artifacts and factshift.center provide a decent surface for exploring the visual consequences of Spw geometry and where expressive navigation would tangibly be desired; it also poses some interesting questions about what it might mean to represent layered information, parallel event streams, loops; how labels may be represented as particles or where the fractal zoom into computational scale may be explored; how Spw may be made more pragmatically computational"
 scene: "someone pastes a .spw document and zooms into an identifier to change its name letter by letter; they spread nodes out and use the tool menu to cluster word parts by camelHumps"
 canvas: "consider hemispheric or quadripartite canvas; consider the frame existing rotated 90 degrees into abstract interactive space such that the intuition of navigation pinch, scroll, pivot dynamics or physical flow may enable some novel design heuristics for both the workbench and its physical models"
 doubt: "I'm currently not convinced by the zooming into and out of document / workspace / variable scale with factshift.center, and I wonder how useful it can be for art or music"
 ask: "consider the novel discovery of symbolic physics, exploration of Spw semantics, and fluency of navigation across interactive surfaces; consider better examples for spw.quest along the planned improvements to the workbench ocean and marketing utility into factshift.center"
 edges: "consider the left and right edges of the frame as objective / subjective or large / small (or context-sensitive versatile)"
 hyperbolic: "some of the scrubbing and swiping felt like it was on its way to describing hyperbolic geometry, and that made some of the moments along navigation more difficult to appreciate"
 ephemera: "the words in the ephemeral UI feel verbose; I'd rather explore a different visual hierarchy and relationship to Spw braces"
}

^"measured"{
 # Run in the workbench at f0bba2d3 on 2026-11-16 unless a source is named. Re-run before relying on one.
 separators: "day{wake, eat, sleep} is said list of 3; day{wake; eat; sleep} is said sequence of 3; with a dot or a space between, the line reader finds one part; day{wake | eat | sleep} says the bar as unread."
 action_potential: "!~ba is said action potential, ba: the ~ is read as a join, not as a second mark on ba. ~!ba is said potential action ba."
 doubling: "!!ba is said action action ba and [[ba]] mode mode ba; ((ba)) and <<ba>> are said as unread, all six characters (the fused n-range digraphs)."
 trailing: "ba!! is said ba!!, ?ba? is said wonder ba?, and chapter%13 is said chapter%13: a mark glued after a name is said as part of the name."
 loop_label: "loop{_A ~again ^_A }_A is said loop, definition, labeled A, potential again, ascension A; end A."
 crossing: "{_A [_B }_A ]_B has one crossing in scanPairLabels, and the spoken reader says unread {_A, mode B, } A, B."
 nest: "day{wake{eat{sleep{day}}}} is said down to day and then end eat; end wake; end day."
 pivot_counts: "@ba@ba@ba has 16 pivots, ~ba~ba 10, ba and baba none."
 pivots_foo: "=foo@(<bar>) has 11 pivots. foo=@(<bar>) reads in the line reader only. (oath_stone)=@boof reads in the line reader, parser and spoken reader. =foo@ (<bar>) reads; =foo @(<bar>) leaves text the line reader does not read."
 dialects: "The parser reads 15 pivot lines alike under all eight dialects; the line reader and the spoken reader take no dialect."
 humps: "Of the 37 query names the canvas takes, 12 are camelCase compounds. By first hump: alpha{Decay, Target}, link{Pull, Strength, Style}. By last hump: decay{alpha, velocity}, strength{center, link}. alphaDecay and linkStrength sit in both."
 word_joins: "node_count is said node count; nodeCount, node-count and node.count are each said unbroken; node~count is said node, potential, count."
 dial: "ACT_MARKS in experimental/pivots.ts is ! ^ ~ ? * = @ # . & $ % (an order chosen as a fallback for turns, not designed as a dial). Half a turn apart: ! and @, ^ and #, ~ and ., ? and &, * and $, = and %."
 wall_turn: "The pivot reader turns a wall concept, scene, mode, body, then concept again."
 tokens: "!save, ^13, @3, #>select and #garden each read whole in the line reader and the parser. @node › #select[2/6] has one parse error and the spoken reader leaves › unread."
 spw_forms: "Of the CLI's Spw-form outputs, doctor, census and inspect source parse; graph has 1 error, form 14, form --resonance 22."
 trace: "named() in packages/spw-seed/src/combinators/utilities.ts builds an enter and an exit event with a performance.now() stamp for every rule, whether or not the caller collects them. The site consumer measured a 242-character example at 41 ms and a 460-character one at 2 ms on the reader pinned at 27fa1e3d."
 pins: "factshift.center bundles reader 2026.11.07-pre.1 (workbench 3f53adf2), before spokenLine (2026.11.13) and pivots (339728cf). spw.quest's parser is at 27fa1e3d. The canvas's main is 1a4157a (deployed); its branch edge-submenus is two commits ahead (b75971f, d93c06b) and not deployed."
 canvas_motion: "From the site consumer's cache motion-and-marks-2026-11: the more row moves the rows under the finger 13 to 17 px; the save button widens 24 px; the press light lasts 700 ms and notes hold 2.4 s; with menus closed the left rail ran files, bodies and lines together, up to 34 stops."
}

^"babble"{
 # Claims to test. Each agent is asked to break at least one.
 separators_clock: "Commas are things side by side (lanes), semicolons one after another (a strip), and a plain space might be overlap, two things in one place like a chord."
 bar_seat: "The bar is an empty seat: fork, choice, or two streams that never meet."
 loops: "Two loop shapes: a label reached from inside itself (a back-edge), and a nest that ends in its own name (recursion as a zoom that recognizes itself). A modulus after a name (chapter%13) could be a loop's mark."
 label_particles: "A label is created at its open and annihilated at its close. Labels are the only construct that crosses the tree, and two event streams written on one line must cross, so crossing labels are how one line holds parallel time. Drawn: tracks, with each crossing a vertex."
 computational_scale: "Zooming into a mark reaches the reader's own rule events (enter, exit, a stamp). The innermost scale exists and is paid for on every parse; a zoom rule would be to paint no scale nobody is looking at."
 evaluation_is_navigation: "A step of evaluation is a line one nuance from the last, a pivot the evaluator picks. On a rail: the left edge where the work happens, the right edge what came of it. Scrubbing back through a computation is the gesture of scrubbing back through ephemera, and a quantity sheet's derivative says how far a small change moves the result."
 humps_sides: "Inside a word the left hump says what a name is about and the right hump what kind of thing it is (English compounds head right), the arms reading at morpheme scale. Hump clusters form a lattice, not a tree."
 capital_join: "A capital letter is a join with no character. Renaming letter by letter passes through other real names (alphaTarget to alphaDecay passes alpha): capture. A gentle rename keeps partial names as visible babble and rebinds when the hand rests."
 rungs: "Zooming into an identifier is word, then humps, then letters. Clustering by first hump is the query link*; by last hump *Strength, a wildcard at the front that nothing plans."
 rotated_frame: "Turned 90 degrees the frame's left and right become top and bottom: before the name on top, what it yields below, reading direction as gravity, potential at the top. Pull to refresh is already that."
 dial_opposites: "On a 12-mark dial the half-turn pairs read as opposites (potential and ground, action and perspective, configuration and measure, wonder and subject, value and substrate, ascension and vibration), and the quarters as impulse, judgment, perception and matter."
 quadrants: "A word at the centre with one wall kind per quadrant; scene and mode are about the observer, concept and body about the thing; a wall turn is a 90 degree rotation. A ring of thirteen will not quarter, since 13 is prime."
 gestures: "One gesture for each kind of change: pinch is scale and spacing (the spacing and attach pivots; a dimensionless group is what survives a pinch through units), a twist turns (a mark by 30 degrees, a wall by 90), scroll is sequence and time, flow is the dynamics. No two gestures for one change, no gesture doing two kinds."
 curvature: "Flat space to appreciate a moment, hyperbolic for overview and nesting, a sphere for returning (loops, labels reached from inside, mod 13). A hemisphere is the visible half of a loop."
 zoom_doubt: "Document, workspace and variable are different kinds, not one thing at three sizes, so zoom between them promises a continuity that is not there. Cuts or lens swaps between kinds; zoom only for continuous things (the force canvas, a waveform, a picture)."
 two_scales: "Art and music hold two scales at once rather than travelling between them (a dotted picture and its scene; one voice at half the speed of another). Zooming out in music is reduction, not shrinking, which a fractal summary already is. A line as a two-speed canon through spw hear; a document as a piano roll of names; a workspace as a mosaic of documents."
 babbling: "The first-touch mnemonic is parentese; the canvas's answer timing is contingency; gestures are manual babble; doubling is the first babble and is where two walls go unread; a babble that lands on a real phrase (action potential) is how babble becomes talk."
 from_recall: "Cited from memory and unverified: contingent replies advancing infant babbling; manual babbling in deaf infants; desert fog in zoomable interfaces; mensuration canons; the infinity series; structural reduction of melodies. Do not rest a claim on these without a source."
}

^"ocean"{
 # This session's reading of the planned improvements to the workbench ocean; not confirmed by the owner.
 what: "The ocean is .spw/biome/ocean, the experimental biome: about 35 surfaces, last touched 2026-08-06. A workbench plan note says the word names five things: a biome directory, a metaphor atlas, a runtime stability channel and dialect, a cache-tier contract, and an unimplemented cluster algorithm."
 planned: "Journal .spw/caches/journal/2026-11-11-one-table-many-scales.spw proposes one distance function and a host-neutral disk geometry kernel (rim, fog, a pivot ring) with the ocean as the home of the code. The site consumer's cache workbench-interaction-theory-2026-11.spw (facet ocean) asks for the same distance function and a field vocabulary hosts can draw. Plans touching ocean files: apposition-cache-granules, spw-beat-diff-precipitation, shape-syntax-ecology."
}
